---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import { caseStudies } from "@data/case-studies";
const meta = caseStudies.find((s) => s.id === "hpe-ai-troubleshooting-agent")!;

import CaseStudySection from "@components/CaseStudySection.astro";
import SectionHeading from "@components/SectionHeading.astro";
import CaseStudyIntroText from "@components/CaseStudyIntroText.astro";
import CaseStudyStatCards from "@components/CaseStudyStatCards.astro";
import CaseStudyStatCard from "@components/CaseStudyStatCard.astro";
import CaseStudyDecisionCard from "@components/CaseStudyDecisionCard.astro";
import TextAndImageBlock from "@components/TextAndImageBlock.astro";
import Testimonial from "@components/Testimonial.astro";

import HPEFirstUseFlow from "@img/hpe-first-use-flow.png";
import HPEProofOfConcept from "@img/hpe-proof-of-concept.png";
import HPESummaryUserJourney from "@img/hpe-summary-user-journey.png";
import HPEUserWorkshopFeedback from "@img/hpe-user-workshop-feedback.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout {...meta}>
	<div slot="after-summary">
		<CaseStudyStatCards heading="Project Resources" columnNumber={3}>
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="Week" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="Designer" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="Workshop" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="Dev intern" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="ChatGPT model trained on our docs" />
		</CaseStudyStatCards>
	</div>
	<CaseStudySection>
		<SectionHeading title="Refrmaing our shared perspective" number={2} />
		<CaseStudyIntroText>
			For years, there was a mad dash to create parity in technical capability
			between our web app and our command line. This focus on CLI parity had the
			unintended consequence of creating a preference towards ML engineers
			instead of creating a UX that supported an ecosystem of personas. That
			alienated a whole group of users, and blocked our land and expand business
			strategy. If our ML engineer persona can’t convince their closest
			partners, they wouldn’t convince other teams to adopt our product.
		</CaseStudyIntroText>
		<CaseStudyStatCards heading="The business case" columnNumber={2}>
			<CaseStudyStatCard
				value="$10,000s/day"
				label="Cost of running a pipeline"
			/>
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="4-7 days" label="Typical pipeline runtime" />
		</CaseStudyStatCards>
		<CaseStudyStatCards
			heading="Important usage stats"
			columnNumber={2}
			marginTop={"var(--space-16)"}
		>
			<CaseStudyStatCard
				value="100s sessions per week"
				label="During pipeline creation or debugging"
			/>
			<CaseStudyStatCard
				value="1-10 sessions per week"
				label="During stable pipeline performance"
			/>
		</CaseStudyStatCards>
		<CaseStudyDecisionCard
			title="Create a UX that resolves events, rather than a technology showcase."
			description="The leadership team came in wanting an AI feature. Research said <strong>10 out of 10 participants</strong> named miscommunication between Data Scientists and ML Engineers as a major issue — and the strongest emotional signal was Data Scientists feeling guilty, not stuck."
			nonDecision="A clean “ship an AI feature” brief"
			theDecision="The right user problem to solve with AI"
			outcome="AI stayed the response — but the problem statement became “reduce the cost of a missed handoff,” not “add AI to the product.”"
			internalSignals={[
				[
					"Cross-functional team",
					"(Product + Dev + me) needed a problem statement that wasn’t “AI assistant” — otherwise scope creep would be infinite and success would be undefined.",
				],
				[
					"One-week timeframe",
					"meant the problem statement had to be sharp enough to design and prototype in days. “Miscommunication between two specific roles” was that sharp.",
				],
				[
					"Big-Ideas exercise",
					"later weighed AI against alternatives. That weighing only worked because the problem was framed as a communication gap, not a missing feature.",
				],
			]}
			externalSignals={[
				[
					"10 / 10 interviews",
					"independently surfaced miscommunication. That kind of unanimity changes the brief — it means you’re looking at the disease, not a symptom.",
				],
				[
					"Emotional language",
					"(“guilt”) came from Data Scientists unprompted. The pain wasn’t “I lack tools,” it was “I’m letting my teammate down.” That’s a relationship problem.",
				],
				[
					"$10k/day & 4–7-day runs",
					"meant every missed handoff carried real dollars and real time. The business case for the reframe wrote itself.",
				],
			]}
			fullOutcome="The reframe shifted what the AI had to do. Instead of “generate clever explanations,” it had to “give both roles the same shared starting point so the conversation can begin.” That spec is much more testable than “be helpful,” and it shaped every downstream design decision — including the log-row selection pattern and the suggested-action chips."
		/>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="It’s inefficient process, not technical capacity."
			image={HPESummaryUserJourney}
			imageAlt="Summary of HPE’s customer user journey gathered in card form on a UX journey maap"
		>
			<p>
				The team had just spent considerable resources deeply expanding our
				documentation, and implementing ChatGPT to parse the docs for our users.
				Our leadership team wanted to use our docs as a training manual for the
				product. The hope was that ML engineers and data scientists would create
				pipelines with fewer errors by becoming more educated about the product
				and how it works with its supporting ecosystem.
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
	</CaseStudySection>
	<CaseStudySection>
		<SectionHeading title="Grooming a BHAG" number={3} />
		<CaseStudyIntroText>
			I got started with behavioral design with my very first design job in 2017
			which was in the healthcare sector. The decision to rework the docs was
			correct, but has an unintended message of “this is how you get better at
			using the product.” What we need for our data scientist persona is
			experiences that say, “this is how we can support you in this moment.”
		</CaseStudyIntroText>
		<CaseStudyDecisionCard
			title="Run our workshop through a behavioral design lens, not a purely ideation one."
			description="I used IBM’s Big Ideas exercise to weigh concepts — but I enriched it with the <strong>Behavioral Design Toolkit</strong> from the Illinois Institute of Technology. The pain we were solving was emotional (guilt, frustration), and a feature-cost-benefit lens alone would have missed that."
			nonDecision="A faster, lighter ideation cadence"
			theDecision="Concepts pressure-tested for behavior change"
			outcome="AI assistant won as the “Big Idea” — not because it scored highest on features, but because it removed the cost of asking for help."
			internalSignals={[
				[
					"Big Ideas alone",
					"tends to reward novelty and ambition. With one week to ship a PoC, ambition needed to be checked by “will users actually change behavior?”",
				],
				[
					"Behavioral Design Toolkit",
					"gave us shared vocabulary for friction, motivation, and ability — concepts the engineering side of the team understood faster than UX jargon.",
				],
				[
					"I’d run this combination before.",
					"Bringing a known method into a one-week project reduced facilitation risk.",
				],
			]}
			externalSignals={[
				[
					"Guilt as the pain point:",
					"people don’t fix guilt with features, they fix it by lowering the social cost of asking. That insight maps directly to behavioral-design’s “reduce friction” lever.",
				],
				[
					"Interview language",
					"kept landing on relationship verbs — “ping,” “interrupt,” “bother.” Those are behavioral signals, not feature requests.",
				],
				[
					"Cross-domain analogues:",
					"developer tools that succeeded in similar spaces (Sentry, Datadog AI summaries) won on lowering the cost of asking the next question, not on raw analytical power.",
				],
			]}
			fullOutcome="The AI assistant scored highest because it changes who has to ask. A Data Scientist who reads the AI explanation arrives at the ML Engineer’s desk with informed questions, not apologies. That’s the behavior change the toolkit pointed us at — and it’s the success criterion the user test will measure."
		/>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="The big hairy audacious goal."
			image={HPEUserWorkshopFeedback}
			imageAlt="Highlight from user research asking “What is the experience like? which is answered with “It’s like having blood work done, and having a a doctor with good bedside manner explain your test results in a way that you can easily understand."
		>
			<p>
				When our users are in a state of stress — hemorrhaging tens of thousands
				of dollars a day, the last thing they need to hear is “get better at the
				product.” The workshop led to this idea of an AI assistant that parses
				the logs, the pipeline specifications, and historical performance data
				to identify why the pipeline isn’t working.
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="Trimmed, scoped, and achievable."
			image={HPEFirstUseFlow}
			imageAlt="The first user flow of the HPE AI troubleshooting agent"
		>
			<p>
				6 screens and a user flow on rails, that was the result. An “explain
				logs” button where users would already be during debugging invited them
				in to quickly get a summary of issues, and provided them the docs and
				actions that they could take to remedy the situation. This would shift
				the conversation between personas from “I don’t know how to fix this.”
				to “here’s what I know how to fix, and this is where I need your help.”
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
	</CaseStudySection>
	<CaseStudySection>
		<SectionHeading title="Proving it for real" number={4} />
		<CaseStudyIntroText>
			With one week on the clock and a single developer as the only engineering
			partner, the team faced a choice that would shape what the eventual user
			test could actually measure: build something that looked real, or build
			something that behaved like the real thing. Design is mostly judgment, not
			pixels — and this was the highest-leverage judgment call left in the
			project.
		</CaseStudyIntroText>
		<CaseStudyDecisionCard
			title="Ship a coded proof-of-concept, not a Figma prototype."
			description="A clickable Figma would have tested layout and copy. I argued for a <strong>fully-coded PoC with a RAG model trained on real internal docs</strong> — because the user behaviors we needed to study only show up when the AI’s answers feel real, not scripted."
			nonDecision="A polished, fast Figma walkthrough"
			theDecision="A test that surfaces actual AI behaviors"
			outcome="Built end-to-end in a week. Now ready for in-depth user testing against the behavior-change spec, not just usability."
			internalSignals={[
				[
					"Development partner",
					"was already capable of standing up a RAG model on the team’s docs. The marginal cost of “real” over “scripted” was small.",
				],
				[
					"Edge-case planning",
					"token limits, model switching, chat history reversion — only matters if you’re testing a real assistant. A Figma can’t simulate any of it.",
				],
				[
					"Trust-in-AI",
					"was the riskiest unknown. The team needed signal on whether participants would accept the AI’s answers, not whether they could tap a button.",
				],
			]}
			externalSignals={[
				[
					"AI quality is non-linear:",
					"Figma can’t show that a hallucinated answer breaks trust for the next 10 real ones. A coded PoC reveals that immediately.",
				],
				[
					"RAG-specific quirks",
					"citations, refusal language, suggested follow-ups — only render fidelity in a working system. Users react to them differently than to mocked UI strings.",
				],
				[
					"Stakeholder demos:",
					"a working PoC is far easier to fund forward than a Figma. The next round of investment depends on what the team sees, not what they imagine.",
				],
			]}
			fullOutcome="The PoC trained a RAG model on internal documentation and shipped the full happy-path flow — log selection, AI explanation, suggested actions, source citations, follow-up chat. The next step is in-depth user testing. One known gap: the assistant doesn’t yet suggest remediation flows (“here’s how to fix this”) — that’s the next-release ask."
		/>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="Real answers, not scripted ones."
			image={HPEProofOfConcept}
			imageAlt="AI model explaining data import logs to the user in a more easily understood format."
		>
			<p>
				Token limits, model switching, and chat history reversion were all
				scoped into the build — not because they were glamorous, but because a
				coded PoC that couldn’t handle them wouldn’t have told the team anything
				real about whether users could trust the assistant’s answers.
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
	</CaseStudySection>
	<CaseStudySection paddingBottom>
		<SectionHeading title="Lessons learned for the next time." number={5} />
		<CaseStudyIntroText margin="var(--space-4) var(--space-0) var(--space-0)">
			This project didn’t get the ending most case studies aim for. The PoC
			shipped on a Friday; within the week, the entire AI Solutions department —
			including this team — was laid off. The user testing that would have
			validated or challenged the behavior-change hypothesis never happened.
			<br /><br />
			What I’d carry forward: the reframe and the behavioral design insight — that
			guilt isn’t solved with features, it’s solved by lowering the social cost of
			asking for help — were the most valuable outputs of this project, more valuable
			than the prototype itself. If I’d known how little runway was left, I’d have
			spent less of the week polishing the coded PoC and more time documenting the
			research insight in a form that could travel independent of the team that produced
			it. Good research outlives bad timing. The artifact didn’t get the chance to
			prove itself, but the thinking behind it should have been built to survive the
			team that built it.
		</CaseStudyIntroText>
	</CaseStudySection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
